{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 3,
  "simulation_id": "sim-04",
  "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B038",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B030",
    "B008"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "B038 remains my preferred achievement. The magic-wavelength lattice-clock idea solved a fundamental limitation of neutral-atom clocks, and its experimental development has made time and frequency measurement an unusually clean probe of gravitation and fundamental physics. Katori and Ye represent complementary and readily intelligible contributions: the defining concept and its decisive realization at exceptional precision. Most importantly, the result is no longer a single impressive apparatus: independently developed clocks have repeatedly demonstrated the underlying physics and its extraordinary performance.",
    "case_against": "The strongest reservation is that optical lattice clocks sit within the longer history of atomic frequency standards and optical metrology. The citation must honor the distinct lattice-clock breakthrough, rather than appearing to recognize all optical-clock development. Some of the most dramatic prospective applications and fundamental discoveries are still unfolding, so the case rests primarily on the completed conceptual and experimental advance.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll emphasizes that independent clock systems now operate reproducibly at extraordinary accuracy, while noting that their ultimate scientific consequences remain under development.",
        "response": "I agree with this balance. Reproducibility across laboratories is especially important here: it shows that the advance is a robust physical method, not merely an engineering record. A completed prize-worthy advance need not wait for every application, provided the new measurement regime itself is securely established."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson ranks trapped-ion quantum computation and simulation highly, while warning that this broad quantum-information history intersects with earlier recognition of ion-trap quantum control.",
        "response": "B030 is a serious alternative because its theory-to-experiment path is clear and experimentally mature. I nevertheless retain B038 above it: the lattice-clock achievement has a more sharply delimited central invention and attribution, whereas distinguishing the proposed quantum-information achievement from previously recognized ion-trap control requires a more difficult boundary judgment."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson argues that high-gain X-ray free-electron lasers have created a transformative ultrafast, atomic-scale probe, but stresses the distributed credit among theory, accelerator physics, and facility teams.",
        "response": "I share the assessment of the scientific power of B008 and keep it as an alternative. Its attribution problem is nevertheless material: a prize limited to three names would need a much more exact historical account of the relation between high-gain FEL theory and large-scale realization than B038 requires."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "How narrowly the historical development of the magic-wavelength lattice-clock concept can be separated from the wider optical-frequency-metrology community.",
      "Whether the committee considers the demonstrated measurement regime sufficient without awaiting a uniquely decisive fundamental-physics discovery enabled by these clocks."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
